#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a852fb-e00b-55e8-bde5-d6f6e2278691" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Function Given a Graph Without Labeled Areas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "830e9698-d539-5352-8d7d-febdec432ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "830e9698-d539-5352-8d7d-febdec432ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517737} :answer-field/id #uuid "27491965-a7e7-5720-b84e-bc2ff10d5755" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f9b4143-02aa-5852-84a2-99409167bad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-131247" :question/problem "Find the value of $f(3).$\n\n$f(3)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(3)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe definite integral of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ over $[-3,3]$ is the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis. The corresponding region is a semicircle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a852fb-e00b-55e8-bde5-d6f6e2278691" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of a Function Given a Graph Without Labeled Areas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1fab290-16e7-5979-bd17-fff2703816f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517822} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e79ac7-4b47-5738-a30f-3fb1606b45fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35ae1f33-1dce-5ef7-af9a-1cf242b3dd81" :question/math-academy-id "q-131248" :question/problem "$f(6)\\,=\\,${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the fundamental theorem of calculus, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-f(-3) \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =f(6)-(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}) \\\\ f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $[-3,6]$ consists of two parts: \n\n* an arc of a quarter circle for $x\\in [-3,3],$ which lies above the $x$-axis, and \n\n* a line segment for $x\\in [3,6],$ which lies below the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,3].$ Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n{A}_{1}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the original integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $f(6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+{\\int }_{-3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}+(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-6) \\\\  & =2\\pi -6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7768eb85-b81d-5f86-b609-5fcaeebe3028" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Increasing and Decreasing Intervals Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4db1f1a3-190a-5cc1-93b3-d9f877fdb7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4db1f1a3-190a-5cc1-93b3-d9f877fdb7e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517639} :answer-field/id #uuid "34a9d8b0-3ced-5c46-80bb-9db0e68239b5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "746f9f87-2e01-5d82-b120-0a1a9057b358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "746f9f87-2e01-5d82-b120-0a1a9057b358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517709} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bdc8cd7-5cdf-51e5-b082-29fd4628e097" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "168e3b6f-08dd-5743-a200-729abea32e10" :question/math-academy-id "q-131249" :question/problem "Find all the values of $x$ in the interval $(-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$f(x)$ is strictly increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,,\\,$  \n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n* If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nThe graph below shows that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-3,3).$\n\n![](images/c7/c72ad85a6998493f29289074a4750f2830e8fdefe51d6f81536257abfb8ae008.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly increasing when $x\\in (-3,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099bbe07-6cae-5cae-80ad-72300adb64c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "099bbe07-6cae-5cae-80ad-72300adb64c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517507} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2db4504-8093-5779-84d9-e20e844db65c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8c45a53-973c-5ec6-9492-ec10ad530490" :question/math-academy-id "q-131250" :question/problem "Find the value of $x\\in (-6,6)$ where the function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum.\n\nThe relative maximum is at $x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n* ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9187b2a-8c34-5dda-8ae4-2c861f1c0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6fa3df3-dbe3-5475-acbd-66da493953b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6fa3df3-dbe3-5475-acbd-66da493953b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517866} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e655be1-21b1-5727-9f22-40f30bd8c752" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518019} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b04dd9-266e-5d29-a13a-aa5b92aacd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b04dd9-266e-5d29-a13a-aa5b92aacd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517825} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9709fc-b41f-5d8a-b57e-49646ab638ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f985c78-b1b4-50b7-b3f8-18827e16fba5" :question/math-academy-id "q-131251" :question/problem "Given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ find the absolute minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6].$\n\n${x}_{\\text{min}}\\,=\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,f({x}_{\\text{min}})\\,=\\,$  \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The minimum value of the function $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\nTo find the minimum value, we apply three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points. From the graph, we see that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and the endpoints of the interval are $x=-6,6.$ So, the candidates are $x=-6,\\,-3,\\,3,\\,6.$ \n\n**Step 2**: Discard any candidates that could not be a minimum.\n\n* The derivative is negative on $(-6,-3),$ so $f(x)$ is decreasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-6)>f(-3).$ So, $x=-6$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n* The derivative is positive on $(-3,3),$ so $f(x)$ is increasing on that interval, and therefore $f(-3)<f(3).$ So, $x=3$ cannot be a minimum. \n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the values of $f(x)$ at the remaining candidates. We have discarded $x=-6,\\,3,$ so the remaining candidates are $x=-3,\\,6.$\n\n* We are given that $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$\n\n* Previously, we found that $f(6)=2\\pi -6.$\n\nSo, comparing $f(-3)=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}≈-7.85$ and $f(6)=2\\pi -6≈0.28,$ we conclude that the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068893 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90ce29dd-5214-5ce3-84d0-3f5c304cc7a0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Graphed Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc78b909-0880-5440-888c-d28056945b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc78b909-0880-5440-888c-d28056945b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517844} :answer-field/id #uuid "e63b5122-3053-56d2-9da4-5e85f3949a91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5064a9ef-4f8e-5fbe-8d7d-2164ebe22645" :question/math-academy-id "q-131252" :question/problem "${f}^{″}(4)=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=({f}^{′}(x))^{′}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that $4\\in [3,6],$ where the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ is represented by a straight line segment. So, ${f}^{″}(4)$ is just the slope of this segment.\n\nSince this segment passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,0)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(6,-4),$ we can compute its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{-4-0}{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{″}(4)=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1331279e-1a5f-5f6f-9104-0c88e1f9f6d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7532c1eb-1150-5b3d-abb1-342bec35e705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7532c1eb-1150-5b3d-abb1-342bec35e705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517710} :answer-field/id #uuid "87ca15d6-48a6-50a1-9d31-763d81685807" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0343bcd3-e974-592d-9f5b-2241e197ca26" :question/math-academy-id "q-131253" :question/problem "Given that the point $(2,\\sqrt{5})$ lies on the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ find ${f}^{″}(2).$\n\n${f}^{″}(2)\\,=\\,$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({f}^{′}(x))\n$$\n\nis the slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x).$ \n\nNotice that the graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ on $x\\in [-3,3]$ is a semicircle of a radius $3$ centered at $(0,0).$ Its equation (for $y>0$) is given by\n\n$$\n(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(2,\\,\\sqrt{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175947 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f73c50f7-fa8c-5d23-8699-e7e074f4251b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Inflection Points and Intervals of Concavity Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93061708-c840-5745-b2ec-76afc08011b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93061708-c840-5745-b2ec-76afc08011b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517766} :answer-field/id #uuid "f041b7b3-189c-536b-a1e4-71dd95312f8d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c7380a8-a7e4-58c2-b50a-6db0fdc55270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c7380a8-a7e4-58c2-b50a-6db0fdc55270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186517607} :answer-field/id #uuid "f11fbe67-fc53-5d48-89e6-804af1e42c3a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51f72acf-5b7c-5f3d-bea9-1b31e5199996" :question/math-academy-id "q-131254" :question/problem "How many inflection points does the function $f(x)$ have inside the interval $(-6,6),$ and what is the smallest value of $x$ where $f(x)$ has an inflection point?\n\nThere are {{field-1}} \ninflection points in total.\n\nThe inflection point with the smallest $x$-coordinate is at\n\n$x\\,=\\,$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ Recall the following:\n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$  \n\n* When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ \n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n* ${f}^{″}(a)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(a)$ is undefined, and \n\n* ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nSo, we summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-6,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign only at the point $x=0.$  Therefore, $x=0$  is a point of inflection.\n\nFinally, the smallest value of $x$ inside the interval $(-6,6),$ where the function $f(x)$ has an inflection point is $x=0.$"}]}
